            <ArticleTitle>Negative Regulation of Skin Pigmentation in Three-Dimensional Reconstructs by Adipose-Derived Mesenchymal Cells.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2464-2466</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-202X(17)32752-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jid.2017.08.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The regulation of site-specific pigmentation in reconstructs used for skin grafting is incompletely understood. Using an engineered skin equivalent model, Klar et al. incorporated adipose-derived mesenchymal cells into skin substitutes and found that adipose-derived mesenchymal cells secreted high levels of transforming growth factor-β1, which down-regulates melanogenic enzymes such as tyrosinase to decrease melanin synthesis and prevent normal pigmentation of resulting skin grafts.</AbstractText>
